Women more likely to die of heart attack if treated by men
The study focused on more than 500,000 patients who were hospitalized for a heart attack in Florida between 1991 and 2010.
Researchers from Harvard University found significant differences in survival according to the sex of patients and doctors.
The study, published in the Journal of the Institute of Natural Sciences, showed that when gynecological patients were treated by a gynecologist, there were many positive signs that could save lives.
Nearly 12 percent of patients died while being transported to the emergency room for a heart attack.
Consistency between patients and gynecologists reduced the risk of death to 5.4 percent.
However, female patients treated by male doctors had a lower survival rate than male patients treated by female physicians 1.52 percent.
Previous studies have shown that women are more likely than men to die of heart attack.
Some experts suggest that it may be due to the symptoms of women being different from men or taking longer to treat than men.
Researchers continue to suggest that a good solution should be to add more female doctors in the emergency room.
